  "strategy": "We are going to improve upon the previous program by adopting a multi-modal search approach. Instead of optimizing a fixed-length sequence, we explore the use of sequences from different scales (lengths). This is inspired by the earlier literature suggestion that sequences of varying lengths may yield better optimization results.\n\nThe evaluation function is `2*n*max(convolve(a, a)[k]) / (sum(a))^2`. We note that `convolve(a, a, mode='full')` produces a convolution vector of length `2*n-1`, and we take the maximum over these.\n\nOur approach is to:\n1. Run the previous program with different initializations and parameters.\n2. Use scale-invariant optimization by keeping the sequence normalized (sum to 1) and scaling down large values to stay within the [0, 1000] bound.\n3. Apply Nelder-Mead local search on promising points found by scatter search.\n\nKey steps in the search:\n- Let `n` be the tried sequence lengths: [1000, 1200, 1600, 2000, 2400, 3000].\n- For each `n`, generate an initial population using diverse initialization methods.\n- Use scatter search (with local Nelder-Mead polish) to explore and converge towards better solutions.\n- Track the best solution found across all lengths and also include variance-reducing mirrors.\n\nThe algorithm proceeds in two main stages:\nA. Global exploration over multiple scales (scatter search).\nB. Local refinement with Nelder-Mead optimization.\n\nWe will use the same `lp_alternate` and `subgradient_step` functions for local optimization, but adapted for the Nelder-Mead method. The primary optimization vector will be normalized to avoid issues with scale.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - For each sequence length, create a population (say 50) of initial sequences.\n   - Each sequence is normalized and within [0, 1000].\n\n2. Scatter Search:\n   - For each length `n` in [1000, 1200, 1600, 2000, 2400, 3000], we do:\n        a) Build an initial population of 50 sequences for that `n`.\n        b) For `i` in range(50):\n            - Normalize the candidate.\n            - Run a local Nelder-Mead optimization to refine the candidate.\n        c) Keep track of the best candidate for that length.\n\n   - After finishing a length, we move to the next length, but also use crossover between currently stored best solutions to explore new regions.\n\n3. Nelder-Mead Optimization:\n   - We use the `scipy.optimize.minimize` function with Nelder-Mead method.\n   - The objective function is the convolution score scaled appropriately.\n   - Constraints are built to keep the sequence normalized and non-negative.\n\n4. Crossover and Mutation:\n   - We use a one-point crossover between two best solutions from different lengths.\n   - The new sequence is normalized and evaluated.\n\n5. Local Refinement:\n   - Each best solution is updated using the subgradient method with a small budget.\n\nSince the objective function is `2*n*max(convolve(a, a)[k]) / (sum(a))^2`, we cannot directly optimize `n`. Instead, we will:\n   a) Assume the best sequence lies in the [1000, 3000] range.\n   b) For each length, normalize the sequence.\n   c) Evaluate the normalized sequence.\n\nWe set a time budget:\n   - Total execution time \u2264 1500 seconds.\n   - We'll allocate roughly 20% of time for Nelder-Mead local searches (max 120 seconds per length).\n   - The crossover and mutation operations are done centrally and evaluated once.\n\nNow, we write the code for the `propose_candidate` function accordingly.",
